.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-stage-create 1  "tapectl-stage-create " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-stage\-create \- Archive and encrypt each unit\*(Aqs newest snapshot into staging
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl stage create\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-\-jobs\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Archive and encrypt each unit\*(Aqs newest snapshot into staging
.PP
Each unit\*(Aqs sources are hashed as dar reads them once, and dar\*(Aqs archive is cut into slices and age\-encrypted in memory: no plaintext reaches staging. Several units may be named; `\-\-jobs` stages that many at once. Every name is checked before any unit is staged. Under \-\-json, an array of one object per unit, printed only when every unit was staged.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = every unit staged; 2 = a unit failed or was refused (the error names each unit staged, failed and not started).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR \fI<VERSION>\fR
Stage this version again, after `stage clean` released it

Re\-stage a specific snapshot version instead of the latest unstaged one — for when `tapectl stage clean \-\-unit <name> \-\-version <N>` already released that stage set\*(Aqs slices and another copy is wanted. Refuses if a stage set for that version already has live slices; use `volume write` to consume them, or `stage clean \-\-unit <name> \-\-version <N>` to release them first. A plain clean releases it only when it already has a completed write AND the unit is not currently below its copy floor (the larger of its policy\*(Aqs min_copies and the sum of its copies_at targets, times [compaction] tape_only_safety_multiplier for a tape\-only unit); add `\-\-force` otherwise — including for a stage set that was never written anywhere, which a plain clean leaves untouched regardless of the copy floor.
.TP
\fB\-\-jobs\fR \fI<JOBS>\fR
How many units to stage at once (1 to 16; default `[staging] jobs`)

`[staging] jobs` is itself 1 by default. The largest units start first, each stage prints a line as it starts and ends, and once one fails no further unit is started. Fewer run when the host\*(Aqs available memory cannot hold that many (each keeps up to 128 MiB of its source in the page cache).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
The units to stage: each one\*(Aqs newest unstaged snapshot

With one unit, `\-\-version` stages that version instead.
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
